OpenStax marks single-letter variable names in ordinary prose ("choose u",
"n is a positive integer", "identify u and dv") with <em data-effect=
"italics">, not a <math> element -- the converter's generic node_to_text
fallback for <em> only preserves italics as plain-text emphasis (correct
for genuine prose emphasis like "reduction formulas" elsewhere in this
same paragraph, left untouched here), so a bare variable name like this
renders as plain upright "u" instead of a real math-mode variable. Hand-
fixed to wrap each one in $...$.

OpenStax also spells the cosine function in exercise 15 as plain "cos"
inside MathML rather than using a function operator. Hand-fixed to use the
LaTeX operator macro \cos so it is typeset with the correct upright font and
operator spacing.

Exercise 25's msup base for (ln x)^2 has several MathML siblings ("(",
"ln", "x", ")"), so convert_script_base's own compound-base rule wraps it
in a TeX group: {(\ln x)}^{2}. The extra braces are unneeded -- a
superscript attaches only to the immediately preceding atom regardless of
grouping, so "(\ln x)^{2}" and "{(\ln x)}^{2}" typeset identically -- and
noisier than every other parenthesized-base exponent in this file. Hand-
simplified to the braceless form.

OpenStax's source MathML for exercise 61 (the volume-of-revolution problem)
splits the boundary-curve list across two separate <math> elements --
"y=e^{-x}" and "y=0,x=-1,x=0;" -- joined only by a literal space in the
HTML, unlike every sibling exercise in this group (e.g. exercise 60), which
puts the whole comma-separated list in one <math> element. The second span
also omits the comma between "x=-1" and "x=0" outright: <mi>x</mi><mo>=
</mo><mn>−1</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn> with no <mo>,</mo> between
them, producing the nonsensical run-together "x=-1x=0". Hand-fixed to merge
both spans into one comma-separated list, "y=e^{-x},y=0,x=-1,x=0", matching
the rest of this exercise group.

This review also repairs punctuation and spacing around integrals, separates a substitution instruction from its integral, places axes and prose variables in math mode, and regularizes boundary lists.

